   "variant_id": null,
   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "Let p be defined by p(x) = k^2 x + 2 for x < 1, p(1) = 2k^2 + k - 4, and p(x) = 3kx for x > 1, where k is a constant. For what value of k is p continuous at x = 1?"
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": 2
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "The left-hand limit at x = 1 is k^2 + 2 and the right-hand limit is 3k.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Equal",
            [
               "Add",
               [
                  "Power",
                  "k",
                  2
               ],
               2
            ],
            [
               "Multiply",
               3,
               "k"
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Equal one-sided limits need k^2 - 3k + 2 = 0, so k = 1 or k = 2.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Equal",
            [
               "Add",
               [
                  "Power",
                  "k",
                  2
               ],
               [
                  "Multiply",
                  -3,
                  "k"
               ],
               2
            ],
            0
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "The common limit must also equal p(1) = 2k^2 + k - 4. At k = 1 the limit is 3 but p(1) = -1, while at k = 2 the limit is 6 and p(1) = 6.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Equal",
            [
               "Add",
               [
                  "Multiply",
                  2,
                  [
                     "Power",
                     "k",
                     2
                  ]
               ],
               "k",
               -4
            ],
            6
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "Only k = 2 makes p continuous at x = 1.",
         "mathjson": 2
      }
   ],
